A||N||19||Black88GT||'88 Front suspension question||||5||
Z||000000||Black88GT||09-07-2000||10:15 PM||||I was wondering if anyone else with a higher Hp/torque engine has experienced the front coming up on their '88s under hard acceleration.  It never happened when I had the 2.8, but now it does with the 3.4.  Anyone have any ideas how to fix it?  I mean its not like the front wheels are coming off the ground or anything, but it would be nice to keep it level.   <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||09-08-2000||07:08 AM||||Fill your spare tire well with rocks, or stiffen your rear suspension(springs and shocks).||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||09-08-2000||08:15 AM||||The front wheels want to come off the ground.  The penalty of power.||||reg||12
Z||000003||3400Fiero||09-08-2000||09:35 AM||||I have a 3400 DOHC in the back of my Fiero, but I cut the springs on all 4 corners and added stiffer sway bars front and back before I did the engine conversion, so haven't noticed any lifting under acceleration or diving under bracking.||||reg||1
Z||000004||turbo86se||09-08-2000||03:10 PM||||When I nail the throttle on my Miller Woods turbo-charged 86SE V6, the front end lifts up a little bit, it's especially noticeable when you let off the gas, you feel the front end set back down. It's cool feeling, watching the front-end set back down. This is with Suspension Techniques lowering springs, Koni suspension, and 16"wheels!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||09-08-2000||06:43 PM||||My 88/3.4/4.10's really lifts the front end up on hard acceleration. But, I like it. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||09-08-2000||08:38 PM||||It's probably worn shocks. My entire suspension is worn out and it will do that with the 165000 mile 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||09-08-2000||09:05 PM||||I'm with Pontiaddict on this one.  That's why drag racers use 90/10 shocks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||09-09-2000||11:47 PM||||Check to be sure that the shocks are good enough,not just some generic replacements;the front end requires alot more rebound control versus compression(3:1).Worn balljoints will allow a little upward twitch also.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Black88GT||09-10-2000||02:08 PM||||Yeah I am going with the shocks, mainly because just about everything else on the front has been replaced!  KYB fronts are pretty cheap too from carparts.com.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||3
Z||000010||Phil||09-10-2000||06:09 PM||||You might want to work on the rear first. It's not so much that the front rises but rather the back drops.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||09-10-2000||07:31 PM||||Jeff,<P>The car just has too much power for you.....you'll have to sell it to me for a song.  I'll come right over and get it.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Actually, I will have an identical car  in a few weeks!  I'm finally going to own my "dream" Fiero.....a black '88 GT with tan leather, gold wheels, 5-sp., T-Tops and every conceiveable power option.  I'll also be putting in a 3.4, but that won't be until Christmas time.<P>Good luck and I recommend KYB shocks and struts.  Use CarParts.com with one of their 1/3 off coupons.  CarParts.com seems to adjust their prices all the time, so the sooner you get them the cheaper they'll be.  I bought some Delco brake rotors for $65 + 1/3 off ($45 total) a few months ago....now the regular price is over $80!!      <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 09-10-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000012||Black88GT||09-10-2000||07:52 PM||||Good for you Mike!<P>At carparts.com they list 2 different types of KYB's, one is monotube, the other is "F GR-2 GAS SHOCK, Exc Electronically Adjustable Suspension"  Whats the difference other then price?  The monotube is almost twice the price of the other one.  Which one does the Fiero Store sell for $80?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfiero||09-10-2000||10:44 PM||||I bought the monotube ones for my GT.....I had the GR-2's on a previous GT.  I believe the difference is that the monotubes are a little stiffer.  I think the GR-2 would be a better choice....at least for me since I have lowered springs.  The front seems a little too stiff.  I had planned on buying a set of GR-2 shocks to see if I could feel a difference.<P>Either way I'd save the $$ and buy the GR-2's.<P>As for my GT.....it'll essentially be identical to yours.....right down to the 3.4!  We'll have to get a picture of the 2 together one day.<P>  ||||reg||10
Z||000014||Black88GT||09-10-2000||11:29 PM||||Thanks for the tip Mike.  I will probably go with the GR-2's, I am on a tight budget.  Do you know of another source for the KYB struts besides the Fiero store? Also, have you ever ridden an '88 with KONI's?  Are they good enough to justify the hefty price tag?  Just curious...<P>  about the GT's, yours is probably in better shape!  I will probably have a mustang hood scoop soon so I hope we can do it before then.  Coming to the East Coast anytime soon???<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||6
Z||000015||mrfiero||09-11-2000||01:23 AM||||Order KYB struts from CarParts.com, but say it's for an '87.  For some reason they don't list the rear struts for '88s.  On some '88 models the strut and/or the spindle will need to be modified to make them work.  It's usually the right rear strut that doesn't want to go on.  You may need to grind some metal off of the strut (where the bolt goes through or the spindle (the slotted hole may need to be elongated slightly).  I have had to do this on a couple of my '88s , but not my current GT....not sure why.<P>I put KONI shocks on an '88 GT way back in 1990, but I never could afford to buy the struts.  The shocks at that time were about $90 apiece and the struts were almost $300 each.  Ouch.  That said, I can't honestly answer that question.<P>I will be on the east coast next week sometime.  I am driving to the FOCONE show in Mass.  It'll be a true cross country trip since I will first drive to Oregon to pick up a car then haul it all the way to Maine (picking up an engine along the way too).  I hope the GT can take it!!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Are you going?  I'm not sure if I can swing down to Maryland or not, but I'll try.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 09-11-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000016||Black88GT||09-11-2000||09:06 PM||||That would be cool if you could swing down!  You could pick up some of these beechwood int. parts I have taking up space (haha).  I am not going to the FOCONE show, its a long trip and I couldn't get off work anyway.  I thought FOCONE was disbanded with the creation of NEFA? Tim Perrigo (I think thats his name) is the guy with all the t-top stuff right? Is that why your going????  A few extra t-top parts wouldnt hurt....<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000017||mrfiero||09-12-2000||12:51 AM||||I'll see what I can do.....I should have the time, but I'll have to wait and see.<P>FOCONE is still alive and well.  I don't know the particulars of why another New England club was necessary, but the more the merrier I guess.<P>Tim is the guy I'm getting the T-Top '88 GT from.....I've known him for about 6 years now.<P>LMK if you need any T-Top parts.  I'll pick them up for you while I'm there.<P>BTW.....should I bring those brakes with me??  That would save a lot on shipping.  <P>LMK.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000018||PJ||09-12-2000||09:48 AM||||The Koni struts come with adjustable rebound. They can be modified to be adjustable in bump. They can also be rebuilt for about $125.00 ea. Excellent for competition use but may be a little too harsh for street use. I had an 8 year old pair rebuilt recently and they are the same as new. They even painted them and slapped on a new sticker! Expensive, but worth it IMHO.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Black88GT||09-12-2000||04:41 PM||||Mike, I sent you an email to keep this thread on topic.<P>I have noticed my back end is kind of bouncy.  Maybe I will go with the struts too, when I get the money.  For now though, the shocks will have to do.  Maybe lowering springs would help??<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
